# Baseline Widely Available, pinned explicitly rather than inherited.
#
# Next 16 already defaults to `chrome 111, edge 111, firefox 111, safari 16.4`
# (see next/dist/shared/lib/modern-browserslist-target.js), so this is not a
# widening of the floor and it is not meant to be one. Writing it down does two
# things the default cannot: it names the mobile and alternate Chromium engines
# a real visitor is likely to arrive on, and it stops a future Next upgrade
# from quietly moving the floor under the site without a diff to review.
#
# The floor is set by what the runtime has to support natively so that SWC does
# not downlevel it: Array.prototype.at lands in Chrome 92 / Safari 15.4, .flat
# in Chrome 69 / Safari 12, Object.hasOwn in Chrome 93 / Safari 15.4. Everything
# here clears those with room to spare, which is the point of using the Baseline
# 30-month line instead of hand-picking versions per feature.
#
# Deliberately not `> 0.5%` or `last 2 versions`: usage queries drift with the
# global stats snapshot in caniuse-lite, so the compile target would change on
# a lockfile bump. Explicit versions do not.

chrome >= 111
edge >= 111
firefox >= 111
safari >= 16.4
ios_saf >= 16.4
opera >= 97
samsung >= 22
not dead
